---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="jetlint">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-eyebrow">jetlint · pre-1.0</div>
    <h1>Type-aware TypeScript&nbsp;7 linting.<br />Without the wait.</h1>
    <p class="hero-lead">
      A fast, type-aware linter for the TypeScript&nbsp;7 native (Go) toolchain. Drop-in
      compatible with typescript-eslint &mdash; the rules you already configure,
      reporting in a fraction of the time.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/install/">Get started</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/jetlint">View on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="compat">
    <div class="compat-pct">100%</div>
    <p class="compat-text">
      <strong>6193 / 6193 upstream test fixtures pass</strong> across 61 rules.
      <small>Same diagnostics as typescript-eslint, byte-for-byte against the published test suite.</small>
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Why jetlint</div>
    <h2>Type-aware linting at native speed.</h2>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Native checker</h3>
        <p>
          Built on the TypeScript&nbsp;7 native compiler. The checker is real &mdash; no AST
          heuristics standing in for type queries. If TypeScript can answer it, jetlint can act on it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Long-lived daemon</h3>
        <p>
          A sidecar process keeps the program and checker warm between runs. The cost of
          loading TypeScript is paid once; re-lints come back in milliseconds.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Faithful compatibility</h3>
        <p>
          Every rule is verified against typescript-eslint&apos;s published fixtures.
          61 rules, 6193 cases, 100% match. Switch over without re-learning your config.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Predictable config</h3>
        <p>
          A single <code>.jetlintrc.json</code>. Unknown option keys exit with a structured
          error instead of being silently ignored &mdash; your config is enforced, not suggested.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Built for editors and CI</h3>
        <p>
          JSON-RPC transport for editor integration; structured diagnostics for CI.
          Stable exit codes so build pipelines can rely on them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature">
        <h3>Open and inspectable</h3>
        <p>
          Open source under MIT. The compatibility harness is in the repo; every rule&apos;s
          score is reproducible on your own machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Install</div>
    <h2>Drop it into your project.</h2>
    <p>
      jetlint is in active development and ships from source. Once installed, point it at
      your <code>tsconfig.json</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`# build from source (requires Go 1.26+)
go install github.com/jetlint/jetlint/cmd/jetlint@latest

# run on a project
jetlint --project ./tsconfig.json`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      See the <a href="/install/">install guide</a> for editor integration and CI setup, or
      the <a href="/rules/">rules reference</a> for the full rule list.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Status</div>
    <h2>Pre-1.0. Stable enough to try, not stable enough to depend on.</h2>
    <p>
      jetlint is currently a research-grade port of typescript-eslint&apos;s type-aware
      rules against the TypeScript&nbsp;7 native checker. The diagnostics are real; the API
      surface (CLI flags, config schema, exit codes) may still change. Feedback on the
      <a href="https://github.com/jetlint">issue tracker</a> shapes what stabilizes first.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
